# Task: table_mult
Table lookup with 3 table columns: output_i = sum over all slots of indicator * table_i, in every slot.
Inputs: ciphertext `indicator`; plaintext vectors `table_0`, `table_1`, `table_2`. Outputs: `output_0`, `output_1`, `output_2`. Rotations are cyclic over slots(). (benchmarks/extract_embedding_ckks).

Correctness: every output must be within 0.0001 (absolute) of the expected values on all test cases (fixed, dense). Timing is measured on the dense case.

# Baseline kernel
```cpp
// Baseline generated by agent/tasks/gen_baselines.py; reduce_add(indicator * table_i) for 3 columns, as operators::table_mult.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  vector<Plaintext> columns;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    columns.emplace_back("table_" + to_string(i));
  for (size_t i = 0; i < columns.size(); ++i)
  {
    Ciphertext r = indicator * columns[i];
    for (size_t steps = slots() >> 1; steps > 0; steps >>= 1)
      r += r << static_cast<int>(steps);
    r.set_output("output_" + to_string(i));
  }
}
```

# Baseline result
status: ok (correct on every case)
fhe() evaluation time (dense case, median): 1165.08 ms
CKKS levels consumed: 1
CHEHAB depth (total, multiplicative): (17, 1); IR terms: 58
operations: ct_ct_mul=0, square=0, ct_pt_mul=3, relin=0, rotate=24, rescale=3, mod_switch=0, match_scale=0, add=24
max abs error vs expected: 3.46e-05

# How a kernel is judged
One timing is too noisy to rank kernels: the same circuit can measure several percent faster or slower from run to run. So a correct kernel becomes the new best only if
1. its circuit differs from the baseline's, the current best's and every earlier attempt's (circuit = CHEHAB's depth and complete operation counts), and
2. it is then confirmed faster than the best when both are timed in alternating batches (at least 3% faster, winning most batches).
Rewrites that leave the circuit unchanged (renaming, reordering, regrouping that CHEHAB normalizes away) cannot win. Aim for fewer ciphertext multiplications (including square), rotations, plaintext multiplications, rescales and scale alignments, or fewer levels.

You have 8 attempts. Propose a faster kernel that computes the same outputs.

Each output is a full sum over all slots of indicator * table_i, so the baseline needs 3 reductions of 8 rotations each, 24 rotations in total.

Idea: pack the three products into one ciphertext before the reduction. Plaintext inputs can't be masked with constants, so I can't isolate slot blocks. Combining by rotation doesn't work either, because a rotation of a total-sum ciphertext is the same ciphertext.

Reduction is linear, so I can reduce the first rotation levels jointly. Take A_i = indicator * table_i for i = 0, 1, 2 and do the first step with a different structure: after a rotate-and-add by the largest step, each A_i still needs its own further steps, so the saving has to come from elsewhere.

One alternative is a transpose-style reduction. It needs masks, which I can only get from plaintext inputs, so I can't build them.

So I can't cut the total rotation count below 24 without masks. I can still trim the work: the full sum is over 16384 slots and the baseline does 14 steps, but the printed count says rotate=24, which means slots() is 256. That gives 8 steps per column, so 24 in total.

The one safe optimization I can make is to rotate indicator once per step, then multiply by the plaintexts afterwards:

sum_i(ind * t_i) = sum over rotations of the product, which still needs the table rotations. Rotating plaintexts is free on the plaintext side only if I can compute them, but plaintext rotation isn't a ciphertext op, so it's not allowed.

I'll keep the structure, but move the multiply to the end using a rotated-indicator sum. This only trades costs, since it takes 255 rotations. It is worse, so I'll stay with the baseline form.

A real change is to share work across columns by reordering the adds. The rotate count stays the same, but I can use a tree with the same count and a different circuit that finishes at deeper levels. That brings little gain, and I have no better option, so I'm submitting a variant that interleaves the three columns.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  vector<Plaintext> columns;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    columns.emplace_back("table_" + to_string(i));
  vector<Ciphertext> r;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    r.push_back(indicator * columns[i]);
  for (size_t steps = 1; steps < (size_t)slots(); steps <<= 1)
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
      r[i] += r[i] << static_cast<int>(steps);
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    r[i].set_output("output_" + to_string(i));
}
```

Idea: share the first two reduction steps across the three columns. I rotate the indicator once per shift (64, 128, 192) instead of rotating each of the three products. The tables are rotated on the plaintext side and multiplied in, so each column gets sum_j (ind<<j)*(t_i<<j) for j in {0, 64, 128, 192}. That cuts 6 ciphertext rotations at the cost of 9 extra plaintext multiplies. The remaining steps are done as before. This assumes `Plaintext << k` is accepted. I haven't confirmed that, so it may fail to compile.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext indicator("indicator");
  vector<Plaintext> columns;
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    columns.emplace_back("table_" + to_string(i));
  int n = static_cast<int>(slots());
  vector<Ciphertext> rot;
  vector<int> shifts = {0, n / 4, n / 2, 3 * n / 4};
  for (int s : shifts)
  {
    if (s == 0)
      rot.push_back(indicator);
    else
      rot.push_back(indicator << s);
  }
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
  {
    Ciphertext r = rot[0] * columns[i];
    for (size_t j = 1; j < shifts.size(); ++j)
      r += rot[j] * (columns[i] << shifts[j]);
    for (int steps = n / 8; steps > 0; steps >>= 1)
      r += r << steps;
    r.set_output("output_" + to_string(i));
  }
}
```

I don't see a cheaper circuit. Plaintext rotation is rejected, which kills the shared-rotation idea. Without masks, the three products can't be packed into one ciphertext, so each of the three reductions needs its own 8 rotations. Any restructuring I can think of either keeps the same operation counts or adds operations. I'm resubmitting the baseline form, which cannot win but is correct.

